**Read:** [Open thermodynamic modelling — pycalphad](../../../materials/metals-and-metallurgical-production/sources/Pycalphad-Methods-and-Documentation.lingua#r_24VWE1JCTVHMZ88907VK19ZWZR), the original paper, current binary and phase-fraction examples, and the original assessment behind one selected database; [Termodinâmica dos Materiais — Cota, 2026](../sources/Termodinamica-dos-Materiais-Cota.lingua#r_05ES8F38ASNQEQMZ0HY6709Z0Z), chapters 7–9.

A CALPHAD calculation combines phase models, assessed parameters, allowed constituents/phases, constraints and a numerical equilibrium search. Open solver code does not make every database open or every assessed region equally well supported. A visually smooth diagram can hide extrapolation or an omitted competing phase.

Begin by reproducing the declared symmetric solution independently. Then choose an authorized public database, identify its assessment/version and reproduce one documented binary calculation. Compare selected chemical potentials, fractions and balances, not only the plot. Keep software, database and conditions together in the reader's reproducible work.

**Work:** Remove or restore one candidate phase and explain the resulting constrained/metastable calculation. Compare a predicted boundary with independent experimental evidence and sensitivity to uncertain parameters. Inspect the software documentation for method limitations and distinguish a historical paper's implementation from the version actually used.

**Return:** [Materials engineering — structure, processing and useful behavior](../Materials-engineering.lingua#r_05BF5JM8141RKF1VT5998YFZQ9).
**Continue in materials engineering:** [Join equilibrium, kinetics and measured material history](../study/Join-equilibrium-kinetics-and-measured-material-history.lingua#r_2PF9SQQQ225YJBHRW9ENRANFEK).

